Now without any further ado, let's start with the first talk from Dr.

9
00:02:17,142 --> 00:02:34,285
Christopher Byrne, who is a Professor of Endocrinology and Metabolism at the University of Southampton, and he will talk about thyroid and thyroid hormone receptor and muscle.

10
00:02:34,285 --> 00:02:51,428
Good afternoon, my name is Professor Christopher Byrne and I'm Professor of Endocrinology and Metabolism at the University of Southampton and University Hospital Southampton.

11
00:02:51,428 --> 00:03:08,571
I apologize that I can't be with you in person this afternoon.

12
00:03:08,571 --> 00:03:25,714
These are my declarations of interest and I give permission for anyone to take pictures.

13
00:03:25,714 --> 00:03:42,857
What I want to do over the course of the next 20 minutes is to describe this condition, non-alcoholic fatty liver disease, as an area for MASL and the importance of metabolic dysfunction as mine is the first presentation within the session this afternoon.

14
00:03:42,857 --> 00:04:00,000
I'll describe the relationship between NAFL or MASL and risk of extra-hepatic diseases, setting the context for moving on to discuss thyroid hormone function in the liver and risk of metabolic dysfunction associated steatotic liver disease or MASL.

15
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:17,142
I'll then finish up talking about resmetirone, a thyroid hormone receptor beta agonist, as a potential new treatment for MASL and describe to you the results of the Maestro-NASH trial.

16
00:04:17,142 --> 00:04:34,285
And finally, I'll touch on issues that are particularly pertinent with regard to this new therapy for the future.

17
00:04:34,285 --> 00:04:51,428
So we were amongst the first in 2015 to describe NAFL, as it was called then, as a multisystem disease.

18
00:04:51,428 --> 00:05:08,571
And in this review in the Journal of Hepatology, we described the relationship between liver fat, insulin resistance, and components of lipid biosynthesis and increases in VLDL that we postulated might be increasing risk of cardiovascular disease in patients with NAFL.

19
00:05:08,571 --> 00:05:25,714
So in this review, we were one of the first to describe NAFL as a multisystem disease that had implications that went far beyond the liver.

20
00:05:25,714 --> 00:05:42,857
Now in 2023, Dr.

21
00:05:42,857 --> 00:06:00,000
Rinella and colleagues and a group of us as diabetologists got together and discussed as part of a Delphi procedure the criteria that were necessary to redefine NAFL or MAFL, as was described in 2020, as part of a disease that had metabolic dysfunction at its heart.

22
00:06:00,000 --> 00:06:17,142
And as part of that discussion, we decided that at least one of five metabolic abnormalities were necessary with the presence of steatotic liver disease to assign a diagnosis of MASL to an affected patient.

23
00:06:17,142 --> 00:06:34,285
And essentially, these metabolic abnormalities are the metabolic syndrome traits that were first described in the harmonized guidelines for describing metabolic syndrome by George Alberti and colleagues in 2009.

24
00:06:34,285 --> 00:06:51,428
Now, when thinking about NAFL or MASL as a multisystem disease, working with colleagues in Verona in Italy, Alessandra Mantovani and Giovanni Taglia, and colleagues in South Korea, Kaichul Seung, Yoo Soo Chang, and Seung Ko Rhee, and Sarah Wild in Edinburgh, we presented the evidence as part of these meta-analyses shown here on the right-hand side for NAFL or MASL, as it's now called, being considered truly a multisystem disease.

25
00:06:51,428 --> 00:07:08,571
And in these various meta-analyses published here, we described the magnitude of the effect by which MASL, as we're now calling it, was associated with increased risk of these hepatic and extra-hepatic outcomes.

26
00:07:08,571 --> 00:07:25,714
And as part of that increase in risk, described the various processes that are going on in patients with MASL that might be contributing to that increase in risk.

27
00:07:25,714 --> 00:07:42,857
Now, MASL, with the metabolic syndrome featured traits as shown on the left-hand side of this slide, waist circumference, an increase in blood pressure, an increase in triglycerides, an increase in glucose, and a low level of HDL, as each of these being a metabolic syndrome trait, when patients have at least one of these, then there's a variable clinical phenotype that exists that differs between patients, but at its most extreme form may be manifest as liver fat associated with insulin resistance, central obesity, an atherogenic lipoprotein phenotype that describes the phenotype where triglycerides are increased, HDL cholesterol is low, and there's an increase in small, dense LDL cholesterol.

28
00:07:42,857 --> 00:08:00,000
There might also be hypertension and dysglycemia or even type 2 diabetes, giving rise to an increase in risk of all of these hepatic and extra-hepatic outcomes.

29
00:08:00,000 --> 00:08:17,142
Now, in 2023, 2024, there's been an awful lot of excitement in thyroid hormone receptor beta agonists because the thyroid hormone receptor beta is expressed almost singularly in the liver and not elsewhere, and therefore the effects of T3, which is the active moiety produced from the thyroid gland, acting on the thyroid hormone receptor beta in the liver is therefore essentially liver-specific, having these effects as depicted here on the lower half of the slide.

30
00:08:17,142 --> 00:08:34,285
So all of these potentially beneficial effects here on the left-hand side might be beneficial for liver disease in MASL, and those effects on the right-hand side here, the effects on cholesterol, ApoB, lipoprotein, little a, and triglycerides might have a potential benefit on cardiovascular risk, which we now know is increased in patients with MASL.

31
00:08:34,285 --> 00:08:51,428
Now, there are lots of theoretical ways in which T3, the active moiety produced from T4, might have a beneficial effect on liver disease in patients potentially with hypothyroidism and liver disease.

32
00:08:51,428 --> 00:09:08,571
But working with Giovanni Taglia and Alessandra Mantovani, we produced this meta-analysis that was published in Gut in 2024, and reviewing the evidence where primary hypothyroidism was defined as L-thyroxine replacement treatment or subclinical hypothyroidism or overt hypothyroidism.

33
00:09:08,571 --> 00:09:25,714
We looked at data from 24 cross-sectional studies and four longitudinal studies with aggregate data on 76 million individuals, and that meta-analysis showed an effect size where primary hypothyroidism was associated with prevalent MASL in the cross-sectional study with an odds ratio of 1.

34
00:09:25,714 --> 00:09:42,857
43 that was highly significant.

35
00:09:42,857 --> 00:10:00,000
And for the longitudinal.

1
00:09:30,000 --> 00:09:41,538
or overt hypothyroidism.

2
00:09:41,538 --> 00:09:53,076
We looked at data from 24 cross-sectional studies and four longitudinal studies with aggregate data on 76 million individuals and that meta-analysis showed an effect size where primary hypothyroidism was associated with prevalent MASL in the cross-sectional study with an odds ratio of 1.

3
00:09:53,076 --> 00:10:04,615
43 that was highly significant.

4
00:10:04,615 --> 00:10:16,153
And for the longitudinal studies then there was a borderline significant association between hypothyroidism and risk of developing MASL over a median 4.

5
00:10:16,153 --> 00:10:27,692
5 years of follow-up.

6
00:10:27,692 --> 00:10:39,230
There aren't really enough studies at dates that have studied the association between hypothyroidism and associated steatohepatitis or advanced fibrosis but in that meta-analysis we found data from five studies and that data suggested that there was a stronger association between hypothyroidism and this more progressive form of MASL, steatohepatitis or advanced fibrosis.

7
00:10:39,230 --> 00:10:50,769
So the data suggests that there is an association between low levels of thyroid hormone in the circulation and liver disease associated with MASL.

8
00:10:50,769 --> 00:11:02,307
And an interesting report from Sweden just published very recently where they've studied 12,000 patients with MASL and 56,000 general population controls and had the opportunity to look at Mendelian randomization to examine whether there might be causal inference.

9
00:11:02,307 --> 00:11:13,846
These data suggested that there might be an association that was confirmed by the Mendelian randomization analysis for low levels of thyroid hormone.

10
00:11:13,846 --> 00:11:25,384
That data showed there that there was a similar strength of an association between hypothyroidism and MASL with an odds ratio in this case of 1.

11
00:11:25,384 --> 00:11:36,923
68 that was highly significant.

12
00:11:36,923 --> 00:11:48,461
And although hypothyroidism was uncommon and seen in only 2.

13
00:11:48,461 --> 00:12:00,000
5% of patients with MASL and 1.

14
00:12:00,000 --> 00:12:11,538
4% of controls, there was a suggestion of a higher genetically predicted TSH level and hypothyroidism that were linked to increased MASL risk.

15
00:12:11,538 --> 00:12:23,076
The mediation analysis that they undertook suggested that metabolic disorders perhaps contributed to 41% of this increased risk.

16
00:12:23,076 --> 00:12:34,615
So metabolic disorders developing after hypothyroidism was diagnosed might be contributing to almost half of that risk.

17
00:12:34,615 --> 00:12:46,153
There was really not enough data to comment on whether hyperthyroidism was associated with a lower risk of MASL in that particular analysis.

18
00:12:46,153 --> 00:12:57,692
So moving on to the data from the Maestro-NASH trial that's generated a lot of recent interest.

19
00:12:57,692 --> 00:13:09,230
The Maestro-NASH trial is an ongoing phase three trial evaluating the efficacy and safety of Resmeteron, which is the thyroid hormone receptor beta agonist in adults with biopsy-confirmed NASH.

20
00:13:09,230 --> 00:13:20,769
It's a phase three trial that's double-blind and randomized being conducted at 245 sites across 15 countries.

21
00:13:20,769 --> 00:13:32,307
The planned duration for the trial is 54 months, but two liver biopsy primary endpoints were assessed at 52 weeks into the trial in the first 1,050 patients who'd undergone randomization.

22
00:13:32,307 --> 00:13:43,846
So I'll describe to you that data as was reported last year.

23
00:13:43,846 --> 00:13:55,384
The inclusion criteria were that patients had to be over the age of 80 and they had to have three of five of these metabolic risk factors, these metabolic syndrome traits that I described to you early that are part of the diagnosis of MASL.

24
00:13:55,384 --> 00:14:06,923
60% had type 2 diabetes and patients had undergone pre-screening vibration-controlled transient elastography within three months of recruitment.

25
00:14:06,923 --> 00:14:18,461
The key inclusion criteria also included at least 50% of the total enrollment was required to have a fibrosis stage of F3.

26
00:14:18,461 --> 00:14:30,000
So that's advanced liver fibrosis where there is bridging fibrosis between the periportal and perivenous regions on liver histology.

27
00:14:30,000 --> 00:14:41,538
And no more than 50% of the total enrollment could have fibrosis stage one.

28
00:14:41,538 --> 00:14:53,076
The key exclusion criteria were for alcohol consumption as shown there, not more than 20 grams a day for women and 30 grams a day for men.

29
00:14:53,076 --> 00:15:04,615
And a glycated hemoglobin level was required to be not more than 9% or 75 millimoles per mole.

30
00:15:04,615 --> 00:15:16,153
So patients with very poorly controlled type 2 diabetes were excluded from recruitment.

31
00:15:16,153 --> 00:15:27,692
Two primary endpoints were assessed then at 52 weeks.

32
00:15:27,692 --> 00:15:39,230
There was either NASH resolution, achievement of hepatocellular ballooning score of zero or a lobular inflammation score of zero or one with a reduction in the NAFL activity score by greater than or equal to two points with no worsening in fibrosis.

33
00:15:39,230 --> 00:15:50,769
And additionally, an improvement that is a reduction in fibrosis but by at least one histological stage with no worsening of the NAFL activity score.

34
00:15:50,769 --> 00:16:02,307
The key secondary endpoints that I want to touch on particularly was a percent change from baseline in the LDL cholesterol as a recognized cardiovascular risk factor.

35
00:16:02,307 --> 00:16:13,846
So in summary, 14% and 16% in the two treatment arms of the study with either 80 milligrams of resmeterom or 100 milligrams of resmeterom given daily versus placebo, 14%, 16% or 4.

36
00:16:13,846 --> 00:16:25,384
9% in the placebo group benefited with improvement in both of those primary endpoints.

37
00:16:25,384 --> 00:16:36,923
So that's therefore MASH resolution plus greater than or equal to one stage improvement in fibrosis.

38
00:16:36,923 --> 00:16:48,461
So relatively small percentage of patients benefiting on both of these primary endpoints but highly significant when compared to the percentage improving in the placebo group.

39
00:16:48,461 --> 00:17:00,000
For the secondary endpoints I mentioned, there was no effect on body weight, no effect on blood pressure and importantly with regard to cardiovascular risk at the 100 milligram daily dose, there was a significant improvement in LDL cholesterol by 13.

40
00:17:00,000 --> 00:17:11,538
9% that was backed up by the improvement in ApoB 100 which you will remember is the key apolipoprotein on the LDL particle and this decreased by 16.

41
00:17:11,538 --> 00:17:23,076
5%.

42
00:17:23,076 --> 00:17:34,615
There was also a significant benefit in triglyceride and these effects in the higher 100 milligram dose are very similar to those on the 80 milligram dose.

43
00:17:34,615 --> 00:17:46,153
Now for the adverse effects compared to placebo, there was mild to moderate diarrhea and nausea.

44
00:17:46,153 --> 00:17:57,692
There were importantly no significant effects on TSH, free T4 or free T3 but there was a reduced plasma reverse T3 level which is of uncertain significance.

45
00:17:57,692 --> 00:18:09,230
Importantly, there were no effects on heart rate or ECG or CVD events.

46
00:18:09,230 --> 00:18:20,769
So based upon that data, Resmeteron received accelerated approval from the US FDA in March 2024 for the treatment of MASH, formerly known as non-alcoholic steatohepatitis or NASH with moderate to advanced liver fibrosis consistent importantly with stages F2 to F3 liver fibrosis.

47
00:18:20,769 --> 00:18:32,307
That approval was based on findings from the trial and also from further safety data from the Maestro NAFL1 trial.

48
00:18:32,307 --> 00:18:43,846
The recommended dosage is 100 milligrams for persons who weigh 100 kilograms or more or 80 milligrams a day for persons who weigh less than 100 kilograms.

49
00:18:43,846 --> 00:18:55,384
It's important to bear in mind that there is effects of Resmeteron when used concurrently with moderate cytochrome P450 2C8 inhibitors and the recommended dosage is 80 milligrams a day for persons who weigh 100 kilograms or more or 60 milligrams a day for persons who weigh less than 100 kilograms.

50
00:18:55,384 --> 00:19:06,923
So clopidogrel and also resuvastatin are inhibited by this cytochrome P450 pathway.

51
00:19:06,923 --> 00:19:18,461
So it's necessary that there's a dose reduction in patients taking those concomitant treatments.

52
00:19:18,461 --> 00:19:30,000
Finally, it's important in the real world now, given that Resmeteron has been licensed for treatment to start to think about identifying patients for treatment in the real world and updates from the ASL, the American Association for Study of Liver Disease Practice Guidance that was published at the beginning of this year suggests using vibration control transient elastography or magnetic resonance.

1
00:19:30,000 --> 00:19:37,594
Finally, it's important in the real world now, given that resmetiron has been licensed for treatment, to start to think about identifying patients for treatment in the real world.

2
00:19:37,594 --> 00:19:45,189
And updates from the ASL, the American Association for Study of Liver Disease, practice guidance that was published at the beginning of this year, suggests using vibration-controlled transient elastography or magnetic resonance elastography for the identification of patients where liver biopsy is not possible.

3
00:19:45,189 --> 00:19:52,784
Additionally, it's important to think about various factors with regard to safety and monitoring before initiation of treatments at 3 months, 6 months, and 12 months.

4
00:19:52,784 --> 00:20:00,379
And in monitoring treatments and responses, it's important also to think about how we should be monitoring treatment responses so that we can make definite decisions where there is no response to stop treatment and to advise patients that it's not worthwhile continuing.

5
00:20:00,379 --> 00:20:07,974
So this is very much a work in progress for the future, but it will be very important to develop treatment responses as to how we should proceed.

6
00:20:07,974 --> 00:20:15,569
So in conclusion, hopefully I've convinced you that MASL is a multi-system disease that increases risk of Marlowe MACE, CKD, and certain cancers.

7
00:20:15,569 --> 00:20:23,164
I've not had time to describe to you how the severity of metabolic dysfunction matters, where there is accumulation of more than one of those metabolic syndrome traits, and how that then adversely further increases risk of Marlowe MACE, CKD, and certain cancers.

8
00:20:23,164 --> 00:20:30,759
And we've published this work just very recently in Metabolism.

9
00:20:30,759 --> 00:20:38,354
I've convinced you hopefully that hypothyroidism is associated with an increased risk of MASL, and metabolic dysfunction mediates approximately 41% of that risk.

10
00:20:38,354 --> 00:20:45,949
I've shown you data where a thyroid hormone receptor beta agonist that is expressed specifically in liver might be beneficial in patients with MASL.

11
00:20:45,949 --> 00:20:53,544
And for the future, we need to agree guidelines for monitoring treatment responses and for stopping treatment of what will be undoubtedly an expensive.

12
00:20:53,544 --> 00:21:01,139
Okay, that was it, I think.

13
00:21:01,139 --> 00:21:08,734
I'm sorry, that's bizarre for me too.

14
00:21:08,734 --> 00:21:16,329
Okay, the next speaker is here, thanks God.

15
00:21:16,329 --> 00:21:23,924
It's Dr.

16
00:21:23,924 --> 00:21:31,518
Arun Sanyal, he's a professor of medicine at Virginia Commonwealth University, and will talk about GLP-1 receptor analogs and MASLD.

17
00:21:31,518 --> 00:21:39,113
Thank you.

18
00:21:39,113 --> 00:21:46,708
It's a great pleasure and an honor to be invited to give this talk.

19
00:21:46,708 --> 00:21:54,303
Go ahead and get started.

20
00:21:54,303 --> 00:22:01,898
Here are my disclosures, an unashamed liver lover.

21
00:22:01,898 --> 00:22:09,493
Okay, so you know, many years ago, we published a paper on the outcomes of a liver transplant for people undergoing transplant for the disease that we now know as MASH, used to be called DASH.

22
00:22:09,493 --> 00:22:17,088
And this is where we put in an organ that's pristine into a person.

23
00:22:17,088 --> 00:22:24,683
And as you see on the right, if you follow people long enough, pretty much everybody gets fat back in their liver, and the captain marker for steatohepatitis is just a little bit shifted to the right.

24
00:22:24,683 --> 00:22:32,278
What this told us is this disease in some ways, or in many ways, the liver is a victim of extrahepatic circumstances that lead to this disease.

25
00:22:32,278 --> 00:22:39,873
In addition, now over the last two decades, there's a tremendous body of literature that shows that MASLD is a part of this larger set of organ diseases that all of us recognize here, from cardiovascular, type 2 diabetes, heart failure with preserved ejection fraction, chronic kidney disease, connected through this root cause of metabolic stress, driving a state of systemic inflammation and fibrosis, with diet-induced obesity as a common root cause.

26
00:22:39,873 --> 00:22:47,468
The other thing that we have learned is that these are not just risk factors for having steatotic liver disease, which is what we always say, that if you have diabetes, you're at risk for having NASH.

27
00:22:47,468 --> 00:22:55,063
But these are really competing threats to life.

28
00:22:55,063 --> 00:23:02,658
And these are all frequently concomitantly present, and we really should be thinking of them as comorbidities that are commonly present.

29
00:23:02,658 --> 00:23:10,253
And because all of these organs are linked to this root cause of obesity-related metabolic inflammation, there is a tremendous interest and there's a scientific rationale for focusing on molecules that impact this.

30
00:23:10,253 --> 00:23:17,848
Okay, so that's where GLPs come in.

31
00:23:17,848 --> 00:23:25,443
So what I'd like to do is review for you the overall clinical effects of GLP-1 in MASH.

32
00:23:25,443 --> 00:23:33,037
Then because weight loss and weight loss-related metabolic inflammation is an important part of the stories, talk a little bit about its effects on steatosis, steatohepatitis, and fibrosis.

33
00:23:33,037 --> 00:23:40,632
And then finally end with a little bit about potential mechanisms.

34
00:23:40,632 --> 00:23:48,227
So if you think about people with MASH, I like to think of them sort of in a pyramid.

35
00:23:48,227 --> 00:23:55,822
And this is the population pyramid of MASH.

36
00:23:55,822 --> 00:24:03,417
At the bottom, where the majority of people live, they are what I would call the low-risk category.

37
00:24:03,417 --> 00:24:11,012
So the risk increases as you go up this pyramid.

38
00:24:11,012 --> 00:24:18,607
At the bottom are people who have minimal amount of scarring in the liver or no scarring.

39
00:24:18,607 --> 00:24:26,202
They may have just fatty liver or they may have MASH.

40
00:24:26,202 --> 00:24:33,797
And their main cause of death is cardiovascular and cancer-related mortality.

41
00:24:33,797 --> 00:24:41,392
So let's just start at the bottom and then we'll work our way up.

42
00:24:41,392 --> 00:24:48,987
So if you target these people at the bottom with a GLP-1, what would happen? So these are data with retatrodite.

43
00:24:48,987 --> 00:24:56,582
And you can see, you know, this is an obesity trial data.

44
00:24:56,582 --> 00:25:04,177
And you can see pretty dramatic up to 24% weight loss.

45
00:25:04,177 --> 00:25:11,772
And it was actually presented at the ADA a couple of years ago.

46
00:25:11,772 --> 00:25:19,367
And what we found here is that in a subset of people who had high liver fat coming in within this obesity trial, that within six months, 86% no longer had MRI-PDFF that is a minimum threshold for documenting excess liver fat.

47
00:25:19,367 --> 00:25:26,962
86% were below that threshold.

48
00:25:26,962 --> 00:25:34,556
And this was sustained out to the end of the year.

49
00:25:34,556 --> 00:25:42,151
So essentially, you can defat the liver with effective GLP-1 anchored therapies if you go in very early.

50
00:25:42,151 --> 00:25:49,746
The question is, if you do that, will you prevent people from developing more significant disease down the road? And that, I think, while we don't have an evidence base, all of the biology tells us that one would expect to see that a priori.

51
00:25:49,746 --> 00:25:57,341
Now, maybe the data will surprise us.

52
00:25:57,341 --> 00:26:04,936
Time will tell.

53
00:26:04,936 --> 00:26:12,531
We'll come back and give you the data when we have it.

54
00:26:12,531 --> 00:26:20,126
Now, let's move up.

55
00:26:20,126 --> 00:26:27,721
So now the next category are people we call at-risk NASH.

56
00:26:27,721 --> 00:26:35,316
At risk of what? They're at risk of all-cause mortality, at risk of increased liver-related mortality.

57
00:26:35,316 --> 00:26:42,911
So these are people who have steatohepatitis under the microscope, but they have fatty liver, liver injury, and the injury is already leading to scarring of the liver.

58
00:26:42,911 --> 00:26:50,506
And typically, we divide the scarring into four stages.

59
00:26:50,506 --> 00:26:58,101
Stage 2 is that magical stage above which from stage 2 onwards, liver-related risk starts increasing.

60
00:26:58,101 --> 00:27:05,696
Thus, they are called at-risk NASH.

61
00:27:05,696 --> 00:27:13,291
The MASTRO trial that we just heard about all tried to capture that population.

62
00:27:13,291 --> 00:27:20,886
So all the treatment trials for MASH currently are either for those who have developed cirrhosis or those who have F2, F3.

63
00:27:20,886 --> 00:27:28,481
This is the at-risk type of population that is defined.

64
00:27:28,481 --> 00:27:36,075
So let's talk about this population with GLP-1s.

65
00:27:36,075 --> 00:27:43,670
So probably the best data in this regard comes from the phase 3 trial, the essence trial that read out just a couple of weeks ago, I think on June 5th to be precise, in the New England Journal.

66
00:27:43,670 --> 00:27:51,265
So although the primary results were presented last fall.

67
00:27:51,265 --> 00:27:58,860
So here you can see same endpoints.

68
00:27:58,860 --> 00:28:06,455
These are standard endpoints for the MASH field, resolution of steatohepatitis without worsening of fibrosis, improvement in fibrosis without worsening of steatohepatitis.

69
00:28:06,455 --> 00:28:14,050
So 62.

70
00:28:14,050 --> 00:28:21,645
9% resolution of steatohepatitis with 34% in placebo, about a 30% placebo-corrected gain.

71
00:28:21,645 --> 00:28:29,240
When you look at the fibrosis improvement, 37 versus 22, about a 15% placebo-corrected improvement.

72
00:28:29,240 --> 00:28:36,835
So these are quite remarkable considering we already know that GLP-1s already have beneficial effects on all the things surrounding the liver from cardiovascular, glycemic control, etc.

73
00:28:36,835 --> 00:28:44,430
, etc.

74
00:28:44,430 --> 00:28:52,025
And so here's now we have very good quality phase 3 data in 800 patients supporting the benefits of semaglutide for this.

75
00:28:52,025 --> 00:28:59,620
Now in addition to this, when you look at the composite outcomes, this was 32% compared to 16% in the placebo.

76
00:28:59,620 --> 00:29:07,215
So there was a hierarchical testing strategy in this trial where we went from the two primary.

77
00:29:07,215 --> 00:29:14,810
If you met the two primary, then you went to the third endpoint.

78
00:29:14,810 --> 00:29:22,405
And as long as you kept hitting your p-value, you could recycle what we call our alpha and keep going down.

79
00:29:22,405 --> 00:29:30,000
And so met this endpoint.

1
00:29:30,000 --> 00:29:36,896
benefits of semaglutide for this.

2
00:29:36,896 --> 00:29:43,793
Now, in addition to this, when you look at the composite outcomes, this was 32% compared to 16% in the placebo.

3
00:29:43,793 --> 00:29:50,689
So there was a hierarchical testing strategy in this trial where we went from the two primary.

4
00:29:50,689 --> 00:29:57,586
If you met the two primary, then you went to the third endpoint.

5
00:29:57,586 --> 00:30:04,482
And as long as you kept hitting your p-value, you could recycle what we call our alpha and keep going down.

6
00:30:04,482 --> 00:30:11,379
And so met this endpoint.

7
00:30:11,379 --> 00:30:18,275
The next endpoint was over here.

8
00:30:18,275 --> 00:30:25,172
This is weight improvement in weight, which no surprise.

9
00:30:25,172 --> 00:30:32,068
Now, of course, as you see, patients with MASH, very much like type 2 diabetes, tend to have a little bit less weight loss compared to the general obese population.

10
00:30:32,068 --> 00:30:38,965
But again, no surprise over here that met the endpoint for weight loss.

11
00:30:38,965 --> 00:30:45,862
And for bodily pain, which, of course, a lot of our patients experience because of a variety of factors, there was a trend for improvement.

12
00:30:45,862 --> 00:30:52,758
It was 0.

13
00:30:52,758 --> 00:30:59,655
04, but did not meet sort of our pre-specified p-values.

14
00:30:59,655 --> 00:31:06,551
So this was not considered as met.

15
00:31:06,551 --> 00:31:13,448
Now, moving on, so we hope that semaglutide will probably get approved sometime this year.

16
00:31:13,448 --> 00:31:20,344
But beyond semaglutide, because it didn't solve everybody's problem, there are still a lot of non-responders.

17
00:31:20,344 --> 00:31:27,241
There's a lot of interest in seeing whether if you add GIP or you add G glucagon agonism to the GLP agonism, do you get additional benefit? So this is phase 2 data, very good proof of concept, showing, again, data with terzepatide, GLP-GIP.

18
00:31:27,241 --> 00:31:34,137
And again, you can see a very dramatic improvement, almost 40% or more improvement in resolution of steatohepatitis, placebo-corrected, and similarly improvement in fibrosis as well.

19
00:31:34,137 --> 00:31:41,034
And this is also moving towards phase 3.

20
00:31:41,034 --> 00:31:47,931
So votatide is one of several GLP-glucagon co-agonists that has actually been studied.

21
00:31:47,931 --> 00:31:54,827
These are data that were published last year.

22
00:31:54,827 --> 00:32:01,724
And now GLP-glucagons are sort of a little bit unique species because they have different degrees of GLP and glucagon co-agonism.

23
00:32:01,724 --> 00:32:08,620
So this balance is a little, even though they're all called GLP-glucagons, they're not exactly the same.

24
00:32:08,620 --> 00:32:15,517
So this is a 7 to 8 GLP versus 1 of glucagon ratio in terms of agonism.

25
00:32:15,517 --> 00:32:22,413
There are other molecules like cotatotide, which are also similar.

26
00:32:22,413 --> 00:32:29,310
And then there's pemvitotide and elfinpegdotide.

27
00:32:29,310 --> 00:32:36,206
So there are already a number of these molecules out there on which we have data.

28
00:32:36,206 --> 00:32:43,103
Time does not permit to go through all of them.

29
00:32:43,103 --> 00:32:50,000
So I'm showing you the servotatide data.

30
00:32:50,000 --> 00:32:56,896
But again, over here in the paired biopsies, you're seeing a really remarkably high number of MASH resolution, and you're seeing similar improved fibrosis.

31
00:32:56,896 --> 00:33:03,793
And if you looked at the intent to treat, this would be, again, in that 60% range of MASH resolution.

32
00:33:03,793 --> 00:33:10,689
So we're continuously pushing the border.

33
00:33:10,689 --> 00:33:17,586
Servotatide is now in phase 3 trials.

34
00:33:17,586 --> 00:33:24,482
The cotatotide data will be presented at ASLD later this year.

35
00:33:24,482 --> 00:33:31,379
And then, of course, we should remember that these are not distinct.

36
00:33:31,379 --> 00:33:38,275
It's a little bit of a continuum as you move from at-risk MASH to MASH with advanced fibrosis because now you're beginning to include some cirrhotics who are still compensated.

37
00:33:38,275 --> 00:33:45,172
Compensated cirrhosis means you have cirrhosis, but you have not yet developed a complication of cirrhosis.

38
00:33:45,172 --> 00:33:52,068
And these complications are ascites, encephalopathy, variceal hemorrhage, hepatocellular carcinoma, et cetera.

39
00:33:52,068 --> 00:33:58,965
So you have never experienced any of these because then once you experience one of them, you're called decompensated cirrhosis.

40
00:33:58,965 --> 00:34:05,862
Then you're moving into what we call end-stage liver disease at the top of the pyramid.

41
00:34:05,862 --> 00:34:12,758
So it's a continuum over here, but over here in this general space.

42
00:34:12,758 --> 00:34:19,655
So we talked about GLP, but don't forget, for people who cannot afford any kind of treatment, there's old pioglitazone and maybe even vitamin E that's out there.

43
00:34:19,655 --> 00:34:26,551
But resmediram is approved.

44
00:34:26,551 --> 00:34:33,448
It's a thyroxine beta receptor agonist, and you just heard an excellent talk from Chris about that.

45
00:34:33,448 --> 00:34:40,344
And then at the top of the pyramid, these are people who have cirrhosis.

46
00:34:40,344 --> 00:34:47,241
So typically these are the people whose FIB4 is over 2.

47
00:34:47,241 --> 00:34:54,137
6, their liver stiffness is over 20, or they have high liver stiffness with thrombocytopenia.

48
00:34:54,137 --> 00:35:01,034
So those are the things that might tell you that somebody may have cirrhosis.

49
00:35:01,034 --> 00:35:07,931
If you're seeing somebody with an AST that is higher than their ALT, either they're drinking or they have cirrhosis in the NASH setting because usually in NASH, ALT is higher than AST.

50
00:35:07,931 --> 00:35:14,827
So in that population, of course, in a small early phase study, semaglutide did not reduce fibrosis regression, although it was found to be relatively safe.

51
00:35:14,827 --> 00:35:21,724
So we get to a point where you're beginning to see now the GLP effects are beginning to level off.

52
00:35:21,724 --> 00:35:28,620
Now the next question then is, you know, yes, all of these different compounds, they talk about weight loss-mediated effects and weight loss-independent effects, but weight loss is a big part of the GLP story, right? So let's focus on the big part of the story, which is how much weight loss did they – we saw how much weight loss they produced, but now, you know, what's the impact of weight loss on this metabolic dysfunction? So there's a very popular paradigm that you need 5% to improve steatosis, 7% to improve steatohepatitis, and 10% to improve fibrosis.

53
00:35:28,620 --> 00:35:35,517
So let's look at some of the new clinical data because that was the original paradigm.

54
00:35:35,517 --> 00:35:42,413
So in the retatratide study, we looked at improvement in steatosis as a function of weight loss.

55
00:35:42,413 --> 00:35:49,310
And where we found the line to start beginning to flatten out is about 20%.

56
00:35:49,310 --> 00:35:56,206
So you get maximal.

57
00:35:56,206 --> 00:36:03,103
So yes, as you start losing weight, 5%, you do lose some improved steatosis, but it really levels off around that 20% mark.

58
00:36:03,103 --> 00:36:10,000
And what is very important is that most of the liver defatting occurs within 6 months.

59
00:36:10,000 --> 00:36:16,896
So there is very little additional defatting that occurs after 6 months, whereas the visceral adipose tissue mass, shown on the right, continues to decrease from 6 months to 1 year.

60
00:36:16,896 --> 00:36:23,793
So the time course of defatting of the liver compartment versus the visceral adipose tissue compartment, not exactly the same.

61
00:36:23,793 --> 00:36:30,689
The liver accumulates fat faster, gets rid of fat faster.

62
00:36:30,689 --> 00:36:37,586
So that's something to keep in mind.

63
00:36:37,586 --> 00:36:44,482
Now what about MASH resolution? So MASH, the diagnosis, depends critically under the microscope on the presence of these cells called ballooned hepatocytes, which are rounded, they have scalloped margins, there's perinuclear condensation of cytoplasm, and these are old Mallory-Denk bodies, if anybody remembers Mallory bodies from medical school.

64
00:36:44,482 --> 00:36:51,379
So if you look at, this is data from the Synergy study with terzepatide, and if you look at the degree of weight loss versus MASH resolution, again, you start seeing, you know, when you get to about 20%, you start seeing 90% MASH resolution.

65
00:36:51,379 --> 00:36:58,275
So again, I think that 20% number comes up as a really robust number through the lens of clinical trials.

66
00:36:58,275 --> 00:37:05,172
And then when you look at fibrosis regression, again, at 20%, you sort of get this highest level of fibrosis regression.

67
00:37:05,172 --> 00:37:12,068
So these are all telling us that, yes, more weight you lose, the greater it is, but then you start leveling off at about 20%, and that's where you get the most effect.

68
00:37:12,068 --> 00:37:18,965
Now, in the cirrhosis trial, we saw weight loss, but we did not see any fibrosis improvement or any real benefit on MASH resolution.

69
00:37:18,965 --> 00:37:25,862
So it also tells us that once you get to cirrhosis with heavy cross-linked collagen and the restructuring and the remodeling of the liver, there may be a point where molecules that are targeting things outside of the liver are not sufficient in and of themselves, and you may need additional liver-targeted therapeutics as we move into that range.

70
00:37:25,862 --> 00:37:32,758
And then we'll finish up by talking a little bit about the potential mechanisms by which all this happens.

71
00:37:32,758 --> 00:37:39,655
So, you know, GLP-1 is a gut-based hormone.

72
00:37:39,655 --> 00:37:46,551
So as a gastroenterologist, we take all the credit for that.

73
00:37:46,551 --> 00:37:53,448
No kidding.

74
00:37:53,448 --> 00:38:00,344
But GLPs are widely expressed in the gut.

75
00:38:00,344 --> 00:38:07,241
They're released in the gut.

76
00:38:07,241 --> 00:38:14,137
But through their effect on the gut, they affect permeability and thus ingress of endotoxin and activation of the innate immune system, a big part of this metaboinflammation story.

77
00:38:14,137 --> 00:38:21,034
It obviously affects pancreatic function.

78
00:38:21,034 --> 00:38:27,931
The whole incretin story started there.

79
00:38:27,931 --> 00:38:34,827
And then it reduces gastric emptying.

80
00:38:34,827 --> 00:38:41,724
And, of course, this has metabolic effects.

81
00:38:41,724 --> 00:38:48,620
It has central effects.

82
00:38:48,620 --> 00:38:55,517
And all of these reduce fat and help defat the liver.

83
00:38:55,517 --> 00:39:02,413
So it turns out that even when you give GLP-1 agonists, and these are data in mouse models, you actually start impacting everything that's happening in the gut.

84
00:39:02,413 --> 00:39:09,310
Now, whatever happens in the gut, always you have to think about what's happening in the gut microbiome as well because there are just so much microbiota throughout the different parts of the gut that the changes in the gut cannot really be viewed in isolation.

85
00:39:09,310 --> 00:39:16,206
So the first thing here is you're seeing over here in the red clusters over here that there is an enrichment of certain clostridial species and other species over here.

86
00:39:16,206 --> 00:39:23,103
And these are data with liraglutide in mice.

87
00:39:23,103 --> 00:39:30,000
But while there's still debate about is this cause, is this effect, and where we land on this, the point is that the microbiome makes a lot of different molecules that are metabolically and immunologically active.

1
00:39:30,000 --> 00:39:40,909
The red clusters over here, that there is an enrichment of certain clostridial species and other species over here.

2
00:39:40,909 --> 00:39:51,818
And these are data with liraglutide in mice.

3
00:39:51,818 --> 00:40:02,727
But while there's still debate about is this cause, is this effect, and where we land on this, the point is that the microbiome makes a lot of different molecules that are metabolically and immunologically active.

4
00:40:02,727 --> 00:40:13,636
And so, for example, you know, you have short-chain fatty acids and other molecules that can go through G-protein-coupled receptors and lead to secondary signaling.

5
00:40:13,636 --> 00:40:24,545
Bile acids, the gut microbiome, are the major modulators of primary bile acids into secondary bile acids.

6
00:40:24,545 --> 00:40:35,454
And these can act through TGR5 to release GLP-1 glucagon, etc.

7
00:40:35,454 --> 00:40:46,363
And then, of course, there's tryptophan, indole, all of which can sort of impact immunogenic, immunoinflammatory signaling from the gut.

8
00:40:46,363 --> 00:40:57,272
The gut also produces things like indole propionic acid, etc.

9
00:40:57,272 --> 00:41:08,181
, which are important antioxidants and have clinical effects in their own right.

10
00:41:08,181 --> 00:41:19,090
So you get this complex footprint through the gut.

11
00:41:19,090 --> 00:41:30,000
And, of course, many of these, the first thing that sees things coming out of the gut is the visceral adipose tissue and the splanchnic circulation.

12
00:41:30,000 --> 00:41:40,909
And the visceral adipose tissue, all of us know, plays a very important role in this whole metabolic syndrome story with expansion leading to inflammatory cues from the visceral adipose tissue.

13
00:41:40,909 --> 00:41:51,818
And as the visceral adipose tissue sort of is set on fire by gut-derived factors, things sort of get progressively worse.

14
00:41:51,818 --> 00:42:02,727
So when you improve that, if you give exogenous GLP-1, you ramp up endogenous GLP-1, the whole axis turns on, and you can actually start impacting that.

15
00:42:02,727 --> 00:42:13,636
Now, as the adipose tissue shrinks, the GLP receptor expression within the adipose tissue also normalizes.

16
00:42:13,636 --> 00:42:24,545
So these are very interesting data on the right.

17
00:42:24,545 --> 00:42:35,454
In obesity surgery patients, where at baseline there's an inverse relationship of GLP receptors in the visceral adipose tissue, which tends to normalize post-bariatric surgery.

18
00:42:35,454 --> 00:42:46,363
And this may explain a return to some sort of a more normal homeostasis within this population.

19
00:42:46,363 --> 00:42:57,272
And then, of course, GLPs have multifaceted anti-inflammatory roles.

20
00:42:57,272 --> 00:43:08,181
In fact, they're being studied now in inflammatory bowel disease, among other things.

21
00:43:08,181 --> 00:43:19,090
So essentially, all of these cool down the systemic metaboinflammatory state that basically drive, which we talked about as a root cause of all these end-organ diseases.

22
00:43:19,090 --> 00:43:30,000
And so this has led, this is my own cartoon of how I think about this, where at the top you have gut-based effects, which involve the gut epithelium, microbiome, and the bile acids play an important role here, that modulate neurosignaling, hormonal responses, and immune responses from the gut that converge on adipose tissue and muscle, allowing them to more effectively burn fat and glucose, thereby reducing substrate overload to all the other organs, and thus reducing the metaboinflammatory responses in these organs, and thus would be expected to improve all of the affected organs downstream of that.

23
00:43:30,000 --> 00:43:40,909
So the key takeaways then are that GLP-1 receptor agonists are potent agents that promote weight loss and can also improve both steatohepatitis and fibrosis, although they have not yet been shown to be beneficial in cirrhosis.

24
00:43:40,909 --> 00:43:51,818
GLP-1 anchored dual and triple agonists are promising classes of molecules with encouraging data from phase 2 trials.

25
00:43:51,818 --> 00:44:02,727
While weight loss independent effects may exist, a substantial portion of GLP-1 benefits are related to weight loss, and weight loss improves insulin resistance and systemic metaboinflammatory cues, driving MASH via complex effects that involve its effects in the CNS, in the gut, adipose tissue, and immunoinflammatory system.

26
00:44:02,727 --> 00:44:13,636
Thank you so much for your attention.

27
00:44:13,636 --> 00:44:24,545
Our next speaker is Dr.

28
00:44:24,545 --> 00:44:35,454
Mohamed Abdulghani, who is professor of medicine at University of Texas in San Antonio, and will talk about SGLT2 inhibitors and MASH.

29
00:44:35,454 --> 00:44:46,363
I would like to thank the organizing committee for giving me the opportunity to speak to you about SGLT2 inhibitors and MASH.

30
00:44:46,363 --> 00:44:57,272
I have no conflict of interest regarding this talk, and as everybody knows, SGLT2 inhibitors were developed in the past decade, and they are lowering the plasma glucose concentration by inhibiting renal glucose uptake and causing glucosuria.

31
00:44:57,272 --> 00:45:08,181
Now, because SGLT2 inhibitor is almost exclusively expressed in the kidney, it was originally thought that this strategy of lowering plasma glucose will have no additional effects or adverse events on the body.

32
00:45:08,181 --> 00:45:19,090
However, over the last decade, we learned that this class of drugs exert a large number of systemic and metabolic actions, both beneficial on the heart and the kidney, as well as improving insulin sensitivity and beta cell function, and large number of metabolic actions on the liver, including decrease in the liver fat content, which is the subject of focus of my presentation today.

33
00:45:19,090 --> 00:45:30,000
The first clue for improving liver function by SGLT2 inhibitors came actually from analyzing data from the EMPAREC study, in which people who received EMPAREC glycosin throughout the study experienced a significant reduction in plasma concentration of liver enzymes.

34
00:45:30,000 --> 00:45:40,909
Here you can see the AHT was significantly reduced compared to people receiving placebo, suggesting that EMPAREC glycosin might improve liver function in people with fatty liver disease.

35
00:45:40,909 --> 00:45:51,818
That led to a large number of studies that examined the effect of different SGLT2 inhibitors in liver fat content in people with diabetes and muscle.

36
00:45:51,818 --> 00:46:02,727
Here, this slide summarizes the results of 14 different studies in which the liver fat content was measured in people with muscle and diabetes before and after treatment with dabaglycosin, with empaglycosin, and canaglycosin.

37
00:46:02,727 --> 00:46:13,636
As you can see, there was about 2.

38
00:46:13,636 --> 00:46:24,545
5 to 3% absolute reduction in liver fat content when the baseline liver fat was about 15%, which sums to about 20% relative reduction in liver fat content by SGLT2 inhibitors.

39
00:46:24,545 --> 00:46:35,454
As anticipated, there was inverse relationship between the magnitude of decrease in absolute liver fat and the baseline liver fat content, such that people with higher initial liver fat experienced greater absolute reduction in liver fat, and that was consistent across all the studies.

40
00:46:35,454 --> 00:46:46,363
Now, this class of drugs, the SGLT2s, lower plasma glucose concentration, and the finding that they also lower liver fat content raises the question whether the reduction in liver fat is related to the decrease in plasma glucose concentration.

41
00:46:46,363 --> 00:46:57,272
To address this question, the effect of SGLT2 was compared to other glucose-lowering effects.

42
00:46:57,272 --> 00:47:08,181
In this case, I'm showing you a study in which the effect of empaglycosin on liver fat was compared to cetaglyptin, and 44 patients were randomized into 12 weeks treatment, and liver fat was measured before and after with MRI.

43
00:47:08,181 --> 00:47:19,090
As you can see, people who received empaglycosin experienced a similar reduction in the A1C, suggesting similar decrease in glycemia.

44
00:47:19,090 --> 00:47:30,000
Nonetheless, empaglycosin caused almost threefold more reduction in liver fat compared to cetaglyptin, which was highly statistically significant, suggesting that the decrease in liver fat is unrelated to the reduction in plasma glucose concentration.

45
00:47:30,000 --> 00:47:40,909
We addressed this question from a different angle.

46
00:47:40,909 --> 00:47:51,818
In this study, which we published last year, we compared the reduction in liver fat in people with diabetes by empaglycosin compared to people who don't have diabetes and whom the reduction in plasma glucose is very minimal.

47
00:47:51,818 --> 00:48:02,727
As you can see in this slide, empaglycosin, 12 weeks treatment with empaglycosin, caused a similar significant reduction in liver fat content, both in people without diabetes, which was in magnitude comparable to the reduction caused in people with diabetes.

48
00:48:02,727 --> 00:48:13,636
Again, suggesting that the reduction in liver fat by empaglycosin not only unrelated to the decrease in plasma glucose, but even unrelated to diabetes itself.

49
00:48:13,636 --> 00:48:24,545
Similar results were obtained by this study from a group in South Korea, in which 98 patients with muscle and without diabetes were treated for empaglycosin 10 milligram or placebo for 52 weeks, and liver fat content was measured with MRI before and after at the end of therapy, and again, was greater reduction in empaglycosin, which was highly statistically significant compared to placebo.

50
00:48:24,545 --> 00:48:35,454
So these results collectively suggest that indeed the SGLT2 inhibitors reduce liver fat content in people with muscle, whether they have diabetes, whether they don't have diabetes, and it's likely that this is unrelated to the magnitude of decrease in plasma glucose concentration.

51
00:48:35,454 --> 00:48:46,363
And the question now, whether this reduction in liver fat content is translated into a prevention of fatty liver related disease in people with diabetes, especially MASH and fibrosis.

52
00:48:46,363 --> 00:48:57,272
And for that, we need muscle biopsies before and after treatment with SGLT2.

53
00:48:57,272 --> 00:49:08,181
And here, the number of studies is very small compared to those who looked at liver fat content.

54
00:49:08,181 --> 00:49:19,090
This is one of the studies that looked into the effect of SGLT2 inhibitor in liver histology in people with diabetes with MASH in this case.

55
00:49:19,090 --> 00:49:30,000
So, and it was a retrospective.

1
00:49:30,000 --> 00:49:44,285
Diabetes, especially in MASH and fibrosis, and for that we need muscle biopsies before and after treatment with SGLT2, and here the number of studies is very small compared to those who looked at liver fat content.

2
00:49:44,285 --> 00:49:58,571
This is one of the studies that looked into the effect of SGLT2 inhibitor in liver histology in people with MASH in this case, and it was a retrospective study in which they looked into people who have MASH proven with a biopsy-proven MASH and received treatment with epigliflozin, but patients had a liver biopsy before and at 72 weeks after the treatment with epigliflozin, and they compared them into a control group which didn't receive nor SGLT2 inhibitor or GLP-1 or TZD.

3
00:49:58,571 --> 00:50:12,857
So they had received another glucose-lowering agent, and the reduction in the A1c was comparable in the two groups.

4
00:50:12,857 --> 00:50:27,142
And as you can appreciate here in blue, the improvement in liver histology was comparable in the two groups, but was much greater for ballooning and for fibrosis, suggesting that the SGLT2 is reducing the risk of more advanced liver disease in people with fatty liver disease.

5
00:50:27,142 --> 00:50:41,428
Another study which has also looked into the effect of SGLT2, in this case epigliflozin, on liver histology.

6
00:50:41,428 --> 00:50:55,714
This was a smaller study, nine subjects only, and it was only a single arm.

7
00:50:55,714 --> 00:51:10,000
There was no control arm to compare the effect of epigliflozin on.

8
00:51:10,000 --> 00:51:24,285
But as you can appreciate, there was a robust reduction in the magnitude and the amount of fat in the liver, as shown in the upper field, and also fibrosis, which here the blue, the staining in blue, was markedly reduced after treatment, 24 treatment with epigliflozin, again suggesting that epigliflozin not only reduced the liver fat content, but also significantly reduced the progression to liver disease, in this case fibrosis.

9
00:51:24,285 --> 00:51:38,571
A well-controlled study with liver biopsy, and this study was published about three years ago, using tophogliflozin compared to sulfonylurea.

10
00:51:38,571 --> 00:51:52,857
They randomized 40 patients into either sulfonylurea or tophogliflozin for 48 weeks, and they looked into the improvement in histology, with histology to improvement in steatosis, ballooning, inflammation, and fibrosis.

11
00:51:52,857 --> 00:52:07,142
And this is the improvement in the scoring of each one of these parameters.

12
00:52:07,142 --> 00:52:21,428
And as you can appreciate, they all had statistically significant improvement, and the magnitude of improvement was similar across all four parameters, meaning not only improvement in steatosis, but also in inflammation and in fibrosis, suggesting again that the SGLT2 is reducing the progression of the muscle into liver disease, inflammation, and likely fibrosis.

13
00:52:21,428 --> 00:52:35,714
Now, in this symposium, we're also going to hear about other drugs, glucose-lowering drugs, that improve liver fat, namely TZDs and GEP1 receptor agonists.

14
00:52:35,714 --> 00:52:50,000
And the question in terms of effect size, how big the improvement in SGLT2 compared to these other agents? And there have been a few studies that compared head-to-head the reduction in liver fat caused by SGLT2 inhibitors to TZD.

15
00:52:50,000 --> 00:53:04,285
I selected this study because it has a third arm which compared the improvement also to glimepiride, sulfonylurea, where you can see here that there was a comparable improvement in liver fat content measured as the ratio between the liver and spleen with the CT scan here, was not with MRI.

16
00:53:04,285 --> 00:53:18,571
But the improvement was comparable in people receiving dabagliflozin or pioglitazone, and both was highly statistically significant compared to people receiving glimepiride, again suggesting that this effect is not related to glucose.

17
00:53:18,571 --> 00:53:32,857
And as I said, there have been several studies showing exactly the same thing when liver fat was measured with MRI.

18
00:53:32,857 --> 00:53:47,142
And the question now, if you put the two together, this study shows that it's even doing better.

19
00:53:47,142 --> 00:54:01,428
It compared the low-dose pioglitazone 15 mg in this case to 10 mg of impagliflozin, and then compared it to the two together.

20
00:54:01,428 --> 00:54:15,714
They looked either at large reduction in liver fat, including absolute reduction in liver fat, or improvement in liver fat plus 20% improvement in stiffness, which is a marker of fibrosis.

21
00:54:15,714 --> 00:54:30,000
And as you can see, the combination of the two is better than each therapy alone.

22
00:54:30,000 --> 00:54:44,285
And more importantly, the improvement in liver stiffness was much more significant and more robust than almost 50% of the patient compared to a much smaller number of patients who got improvement in both liver fat and fibrosis and stiffness, showing that indeed this class of drugs is really improving the development of liver disease, including fibrosis and likely cirrhosis.

23
00:54:44,285 --> 00:54:58,571
And that's a big question, whether these beneficial metabolic effects on liver fat or some cases in histology are translated into better liver outcome studies.

24
00:54:58,571 --> 00:55:12,857
So in other words, what is the long-term effect of SUT2 inhibitors in the liver outcome? And this study is from the UK, in which they collected all patients with type 2 diabetes who were started on SUT2 inhibitors between the period of 2007 and 2021, and compared them to all patients who were started on DPP4 inhibitor.

25
00:55:12,857 --> 00:55:27,142
And they looked into the outcome with cirrhosis, decompensated cirrhosis, hepatic carcinoma, or hepatic death.

26
00:55:27,142 --> 00:55:41,428
And as you can see, all people who received SUT2 inhibitors compared to DPP4 inhibitor, there was a 36 reduction in the incidence of cirrhosis.

27
00:55:41,428 --> 00:55:55,714
Likewise, a highly statistically significant reduction in decompensated cirrhosis, which was 26%.

28
00:55:55,714 --> 00:56:10,000
There was also tendency toward less hepatic carcinoma and liver death, but didn't reach statistical significance.

29
00:56:10,000 --> 00:56:24,285
And they attribute that probably because these are more distal complications of the disease, and they required a longer follow-up in order to become statistically significant.

30
00:56:24,285 --> 00:56:38,571
Interesting, in the same study, they didn't observe any effect of G1 receptor agonist, but I will not discuss that.

31
00:56:38,571 --> 00:56:52,857
A second study that looked into the effect of SUT2 inhibitors, this was a national database from Korea, in which they took all of the people who have diagnosed with muscle, and then looked into the incidence of what they call hepatic event, and they defined hepatic event as an ascites, esophageal viruses, with bleeding, hepatic failure, or liver transplant.

32
00:56:52,857 --> 00:57:07,142
And then they compared the patients who were started on SUT2, the incidence of these events, in people started with SUT2 versus G1, or started on SUT2 versus people who were started on TZDs.

33
00:57:07,142 --> 00:57:21,428
And as you can see, there was a lower incidence of these hepatic events in both with G1 or on those who started with TZDs.

34
00:57:21,428 --> 00:57:35,714
And now, because these are hemodynamic-related events, like ascites, like viruses, and definitely bleeding from viruses, so no wonder that reduction with SUT2 was so prominent, and compared to another beneficial effects of two drugs that indeed reduced the liver fat content, like the TZDs and G1 receptor agonist.

35
00:57:35,714 --> 00:57:50,000
But these results suggest that indeed the reduction in liver fat caused by SUT2 inhibitor is really translated into less incidence or lower risk of having liver disease.

36
00:57:50,000 --> 00:58:04,285
Similar results were shown in this study, probably you have seen it in the previous presentation, in a paper which just came up, in which they looked into a database from 17 or 16 centers, and this case is not only from South Asian, but also included European and Western patients.

37
00:58:04,285 --> 00:58:18,571
And about a third of the patient received SUT2 inhibitors, and they looked into both liver-related events, like what I showed you in the previous study, and also into progression of liver stiffness as measured with a fibroscan.

38
00:58:18,571 --> 00:58:32,857
And as you can see here, both the liver-related events and the liver stiffness were highly reduced in people who received SUT2 inhibitors versus people who did not receive SUT2 inhibitor, suggesting again that the use of SUT2 inhibitors not only reduced the liver fat, but also reduced the risk of progression to liver disease and liver outcome, which even can reach up to transplantation and death.

39
00:58:32,857 --> 00:58:47,142
And the big question now, how the SUT2s are doing this? What is the mechanism behind the beneficial effect of SUT2 in the liver? A large question is whether this is a direct effect or it's an indirect effect.

40
00:58:47,142 --> 00:59:01,428
It's universally accepted that the SUT2 is almost exclusively expressed in the kidney.

41
00:59:01,428 --> 00:59:15,714
And whether it's present in other tissues is debatable, primarily due to lack of good antibodies that can detect in a reliable way the presence of the SUT2 transporter in other tissues.

42
00:59:15,714 --> 00:59:30,000
Now, in recent years, there have been reports showing that the SUT2 inhibitor.

1
00:59:30,000 --> 00:59:39,375
It's an indirect effect.

2
00:59:39,375 --> 00:59:48,750
It's universally accepted that the SGLT2 is almost exclusively expressed in the kidney.

3
00:59:48,750 --> 00:59:58,125
And whether it's present in other tissues is debatable, primarily due to lack of good antibodies that can detect with a reliable way the presence of the SGLT2 transporter in other tissues.

4
00:59:58,125 --> 01:00:07,500
Now, in recent years, there have been reports showing that the SGLT2 inhibitor is expressed in several cancerous cells, in which it provides additional source of glucose coming into the cells, including liver cancer cells, also thyroid cells, and prostate cancer cells that also have been shown to express SGLT2 inhibitors.

5
01:00:07,500 --> 01:00:16,875
In this study, the investigators took liver tissue from people with chronic liver disease who were taken for diagnosis, and they compared them into a tissue taken from autopsy from kidney, heart, spleen, and brain.

6
01:00:16,875 --> 01:00:26,250
And as you can see, they were able to detect the presence of SGLT2 inhibitor in the liver of patients with chronic disease as well as in the heart and the spleen.

7
01:00:26,250 --> 01:00:35,625
And they want to suggest that at least some of the beneficial effects of this class of drugs, the SGLT2 inhibitors, is due to inhibition of glucose entrance into the liver cells through this transporter.

8
01:00:35,625 --> 01:00:45,000
As everybody knows, GLUT2 is the major source of influx of glucose into the cell.

9
01:00:45,000 --> 01:00:54,375
How much inhibiting the SGLT2 in those cells that express the SGLT2 inhibitor reduce the glucose influx into the cell remains to be seen.

10
01:00:54,375 --> 01:01:03,750
But at least the authors suggest that this reduction leads to decrease in the ATP level in those cells and activation of AMP kinase, which lead to all kinds of metabolic activation inside the cells, including influence on liver fat synthesis and other metabolic benefits.

11
01:01:03,750 --> 01:01:13,125
Now, I'll come to that later, but even if there is no SGLT2 transporter expressed in liver cells, there are indirect action, metabolic action, systemic action that this class of drugs, the SGLT2s, cause that might explain the reduction in liver fat.

12
01:01:13,125 --> 01:01:22,500
One is weight loss, which is universally accepted that improve liver fat, and secondly, improving insulin sensitivity.

13
01:01:22,500 --> 01:01:31,875
And here in the study that I showed you, both were related in terms to the amount of reduction in liver fat.

14
01:01:31,875 --> 01:01:41,250
A third effect that also can explain that is improvement in fat oxidation, increase in fat oxidation based on the fasting state as well as after insulin infusion.

15
01:01:41,250 --> 01:01:50,625
In this study, adding dabagliflozin increased fat oxidation and definitely increased fat oxidation can lead to a reduction in liver fat.

16
01:01:50,625 --> 01:02:00,000
And as you can see here, it was associated with increased ketone production, which is made in the liver, suggesting that hepatic fat oxidation is also increased.

17
01:02:00,000 --> 01:02:09,375
Now, regarding to the inhibition of complex I and the activation of AMP kinase, there are a lot of arguments against this because metformin does that and it doesn't affect liver fat.

18
01:02:09,375 --> 01:02:18,750
This canagliflozin, which was reported to be more efficient in this inhibiting complex I and activating AMP kinase, has similar effect to impactliflozin and dabagliflozin on liver fat content, and therefore it's unlikely that this is the mechanism.

19
01:02:18,750 --> 01:02:28,125
Thirdly, these studies were done in tissue culture where the concentration used is three-quarter of magnitude than those achieved in the plasma.

20
01:02:28,125 --> 01:02:37,500
And if that inhibition of complex I, what mediates the in vivo action, it should lead to increase in plasma lactate, but these drugs actually reduce plasma lactate.

21
01:02:37,500 --> 01:02:46,875
In the last minute or so, I want to focus on one important factor, which I think is underappreciated, and that is the portal hyperinsulinemia because the increase in liver fat content primarily has been shown to be due to increase in de novo lipogenesis and increase in production of triglycerides, and portal hyperinsulinemia is an important factor that is driving de novo lipogenesis.

22
01:02:46,875 --> 01:02:56,250
And to demonstrate that, this study that compared liver fat content in people with type 1 diabetes to normal individuals who were matched with BMI and insulin sensitivity, and of course those with type 1 diabetes, they have much lower C-peptide, which is indicative of portal insulin compared to normal.

23
01:02:56,250 --> 01:03:05,625
Nonetheless, people with type 1 diabetes had a huge reduction in liver fat content compared to people, to normal individuals.

24
01:03:05,625 --> 01:03:15,000
And likewise, we compared that also in an ongoing study in Basman in Kuwait that we are doing to people with normal glucose tolerance and people to type 2 diabetes who had similar glucose control to those with type 1 diabetes, and all three groups were matched with BMI, as you can see here.

25
01:03:15,000 --> 01:03:24,375
And you can appreciate the amount of liver fat is significantly lower than those in NGT, those with type 2 diabetes, suggesting that depleting portal hyperinsulinemia is beneficial to liver fat.

26
01:03:24,375 --> 01:03:33,750
And as I showed you before, the SGLT2 inhibitor significantly lower insulin, plasma insulin concentration, and what I think is also contributing to the reduction in de novo lipogenesis.

27
01:03:33,750 --> 01:03:43,125
So to summarize, this class of drugs is promising for reduction in liver fat content.

28
01:03:43,125 --> 01:03:52,500
We need more studies with histology to demonstrate that indeed inflammation and fibrosis is also reduced, and there is a long-term study evidence to suggest that long-term treatment with this drug reduced liver outcomes, especially if they are hemodynamically related, and multiple mechanisms are likely to contribute.

29
01:03:52,500 --> 01:04:01,875
And when we say multiple mechanisms, probably we don't know the exact mechanism.

30
01:04:01,875 --> 01:04:11,250
Thank you very much for your attention.

31
01:04:11,250 --> 01:04:20,625
Our fourth speaker is Dr.

32
01:04:20,625 --> 01:04:30,000
Renata Belforte de Aguiar, who is an associate professor at the University of Texas in San Antonio, and she will talk about pioglitazone and NAFT.

33
01:04:30,000 --> 01:04:39,375
Hi, good afternoon.

34
01:04:39,375 --> 01:04:48,750
Thanks for the organizers to invite me here to speak today about pioglitazone and NAFLD.

35
01:04:48,750 --> 01:04:58,125
I feel like I'm going back in time here.

36
01:04:58,125 --> 01:05:07,500
No conflicts, and it's okay to take pictures.

37
01:05:07,500 --> 01:05:16,875
So I'm not going to spend too much time.

38
01:05:16,875 --> 01:05:26,250
I'm the last speaker in the session, and it has been discussed already about the genesis of MASH and NAFLD.

39
01:05:26,250 --> 01:05:35,625
But I just want to, for the endocrinologists in the audience, I want to mention that for us, several disorders associated with MASH and NAFLD, sorry, NAFLD and MASH, I'm going to use the new terms, right? For hypogonadism, PCOS, hypothyroidism, growth hormone deficiency.

40
01:05:35,625 --> 01:05:45,000
But most important, obesity and type 2 diabetes that was mentioned before, they are not only associated with increased incidence of NAFLD, but also progression to MASH and cirrhosis.

41
01:05:45,000 --> 01:05:54,375
So it's very important for this audience.

42
01:05:54,375 --> 01:06:03,750
So as I said, I'm going to go back a little bit on time, and I want to go over the history of NAFLD and then why this is important, putting together with the pioglitazone.

43
01:06:03,750 --> 01:06:13,125
So NAFLD was recognized as a disease in 1962, and it was named non-alcoholic steatohepatitis in around 1980.

44
01:06:13,125 --> 01:06:22,500
And at that time, it was already found to be associated with obesity, diabetes, and dyslipidemia.

45
01:06:22,500 --> 01:06:31,875
In the late 1990s, early 2000s, it was associated with insulin resistance.

46
01:06:31,875 --> 01:06:41,250
And I just want to make a disclosure that I'm going to talk about NAFLD and NASH, use some of the old terminologies because of the literature that I'm presenting.

47
01:06:41,250 --> 01:06:50,625
So some of the initial studies show an association, I'm going to show here.

48
01:06:50,625 --> 01:07:00,000
So NAFLD is associated with grading insulin resistance.

49
01:07:00,000 --> 01:07:09,375
In this study, they investigated 46 patients with NAFLD.

50
01:07:09,375 --> 01:07:18,750
They rule out presence of other chronic disease.

51
01:07:18,750 --> 01:07:28,125
Patients only were diagnosed with NAFLD based on the ultrasound finds.

52
01:07:28,125 --> 01:07:37,500
And none have hyperglycemia in this study.

53
01:07:37,500 --> 01:07:46,875
They did OGTTs.

54
01:07:46,875 --> 01:07:56,250
And they compared with a group of 92 patients with control without NAFLD.

55
01:07:56,250 --> 01:08:05,625
And there was no glucose levels were high in this group.

56
01:08:05,625 --> 01:08:15,000
Insulin levels were also higher.

57
01:08:15,000 --> 01:08:24,375
And they observed insulin resistance, which was calculated by HOMA.

58
01:08:24,375 --> 01:08:33,750
It was significantly higher in this group in the patients with NAFLD.

59
01:08:33,750 --> 01:08:43,125
In a similar study in 2001, using the gold standard test for measuring insulin resistance, the authors demonstrated that in control, six controls, six with NAFLD and six with NASH, there was an increase in insulin resistance, which was stepwise and significantly lower in patients with NASH.

60
01:08:43,125 --> 01:08:52,500
So with that, there was some initially some thought to start using insulin sensitizers for the treatment of fatty liver disease.

61
01:08:52,500 --> 01:09:01,875
And the first pilot trials came out around 2003, 2005, 2004.

62
01:09:01,875 --> 01:09:11,250
And at the same time, some of the first insulin sensitizers were released in the market, triglyterazone, if you remember that, 1997, was approved by the FDA.

63
01:09:11,250 --> 01:09:20,625
But it came out of the market in 2000 due to severe liver toxicity.

64
01:09:20,625 --> 01:09:30,000
In 1999, pioglitazone and rosiglitazone were approved by the FDA.

1
01:09:30,000 --> 01:09:38,695
And the first pilot trials came out around 2003-2005, 2004.

2
01:09:38,695 --> 01:09:47,391
And at the same time, some of the first insulin sensitizers were released in the market through glitter zone, if you remember that, 1997, was approved by the FDA.

3
01:09:47,391 --> 01:09:56,086
But it came out of the market in 2000 due to severe liver toxicity.

4
01:09:56,086 --> 01:10:04,782
In 1999, pioglitazone and rosiglitazone were approved by the FDA.

5
01:10:04,782 --> 01:10:13,478
And the first randomized control trials came around 2004 and 2008.

6
01:10:13,478 --> 01:10:22,173
So pioglitazone, for the younger generation that hasn't maybe used those drugs that much, so it's a member of the TZDs, thiazolidin and ion class.

7
01:10:22,173 --> 01:10:30,869
It's the only insulin sensitizer approved for treatment of type 2 diabetes.

8
01:10:30,869 --> 01:10:39,565
And it's used as off-label for MASH.

9
01:10:39,565 --> 01:10:48,260
It's a ligand of the nuclear receptor, nuclear peroxisomal proliferate activated receptors, or PPARs.

10
01:10:48,260 --> 01:10:56,956
And PPARs are members of the nuclear receptor superfamily of ligand-inducible transcription factors.

11
01:10:56,956 --> 01:11:05,652
So the PPARs, they have a site where they are activated by fatty acids or synthetic ligands like TZD.

12
01:11:05,652 --> 01:11:14,347
And when they bind to the nuclear receptor, they regulate target gene expression.

13
01:11:14,347 --> 01:11:23,043
And some of the genes are involved with lipid metabolism, insulin sensitivity, inflammation, and self-proliferation.

14
01:11:23,043 --> 01:11:31,739
The activator of the PPARs, pioglitazone is an activator of the PPAR-gamma, and it's a weak PPAR-alpha agonist.

15
01:11:31,739 --> 01:11:40,434
And this makes a difference because depending on the localization, you can have different effects.

16
01:11:40,434 --> 01:11:49,130
So the PPAR-gammas are usually found in adipose tissue, pancreatic beta cells, macrophages, vascular endothelium, and the central nervous system, while PPAR-alpha is found in the liver, heart, skeletal muscle, and vascular wall.

17
01:11:49,130 --> 01:11:57,826
I want to mention that rosiglitazone only binds to the PPAR-gamma, and that might be one of the reasons why we see different effects from the PPAR-rosiglitazone and pioglitazone.

18
01:11:57,826 --> 01:12:06,521
I don't need to talk too much about the pathogenesis of type 2 diabetes, but we know there are several organs that are involved.

19
01:12:06,521 --> 01:12:15,217
And the pioglitazone has an effect that can improve several of these steps.

20
01:12:15,217 --> 01:12:23,913
So it is shown to improve hepatic glucose production, decrease lipolysis, improve inflammation, increase beta cell function, and in the muscle, as it was released, the reason why it was created, it improves muscle glucose uptake.

21
01:12:23,913 --> 01:12:32,608
Just to kind of review the dose, initially you start with 15 milligrams per day, and the max dose is 45.

22
01:12:32,608 --> 01:12:41,304
Usually you can wait 4 to 12 weeks to increase the dose.

23
01:12:41,304 --> 01:12:50,000
But it's important to know that 80% of the effect is already seen with 30 milligrams per day, and you can see less side effects with that dose.

24
01:12:50,000 --> 01:12:58,695
One of the big benefits compared with all the studies that came out, all the talks that came before, is that it's a generic drug and very low cost.

25
01:12:58,695 --> 01:13:07,391
So if cost is an issue in terms of prescribing this drug, deciding treatment for patients with diabetes, that would be a good reason to consider that.

26
01:13:07,391 --> 01:13:16,086
So I want to go over a few of the clinical trials with pioglitazone and mesodine.

27
01:13:16,086 --> 01:13:24,782
So this first pilot trial in 2004, so they used pioglitazone 30 milligrams per day for 48 weeks.

28
01:13:24,782 --> 01:13:33,478
And this was a known control.

29
01:13:33,478 --> 01:13:42,173
There was an open label, so all the patients received pioglitazone.

30
01:13:42,173 --> 01:13:50,869
And you can see here on this graph, the ALT levels were lower, and they kind of normalized around 24 weeks and remained within the normal range until the end of the study around 48 weeks.

31
01:13:50,869 --> 01:13:59,565
So the histological finds from the study, they saw there was an improvement in NASH with decreasing histiatosis, hepatocellular injury, inflammation, and they also saw a significant decrease in fibros.

32
01:13:59,565 --> 01:14:08,260
As I said, this was a known control study.

33
01:14:08,260 --> 01:14:16,956
So this is one of the first studies that was published using TZDs as a randomized control trial.

34
01:14:16,956 --> 01:14:25,652
So in this study, our group investigated 21 patients treated with placebo, and they had 26 patients treated with pioglitazone.

35
01:14:25,652 --> 01:14:34,347
I just want to mention that all patients met with the dietician, and they were told to follow a low-calorie diet, cutting down 500 calories per day.

36
01:14:34,347 --> 01:14:43,043
We also had 10 healthy controls.

37
01:14:43,043 --> 01:14:51,739
The placebo and pioglitazone group were well-matched for age, sex, and body mass index.

38
01:14:51,739 --> 01:15:00,434
And you can see here with the placebo group, there was no change in none of those parameters, while the pioglitazone reduced plasma glucose, A1C, and fasting plasma insulin.

39
01:15:00,434 --> 01:15:09,130
However, there was a small increase in weight of around 2.

40
01:15:09,130 --> 01:15:17,826
5 kilos.

41
01:15:17,826 --> 01:15:26,521
Pioglitazone was shown in this study to reduce liver fat content, and you can see here placebo, there was no change, while the pioglitazone resulted in a significant decrease in liver fat content, and this was significant compared to the control group.

42
01:15:26,521 --> 01:15:35,217
The hepatic insulin sensitivity was calculated using 3D-8 glucose and multiplied by insulin levels, and hepatic insulin sensitivity did not significantly change in the placebo group, while it increased with pioglitazone, and that was also significant comparing the two arms in the study.

43
01:15:35,217 --> 01:15:43,913
We had biopsy done at baseline and at six months, and you can see here the criteria for the NASH activity score, inflammation, necrosis, and dysthetosis, they all reduced with pioglitazone.

44
01:15:43,913 --> 01:15:52,608
Fibros also, there was a small decrease, but that was not significant when compared to the two groups.

45
01:15:52,608 --> 01:16:01,304
There was a trend with p-value of 0.

46
01:16:01,304 --> 01:16:10,000
08.

47
01:16:10,000 --> 01:16:18,695
After that, several other studies came back, came out looking at the effect of pioglitazone, and they all show pretty much similar results showing some improvement in dysthetosis and inflammation.

48
01:16:18,695 --> 01:16:27,391
I want to just focus a little longer on this study published in 2006, which they follow patients with pioglitazone for up to three years, so it's one of the longest studies so far.

49
01:16:27,391 --> 01:16:36,086
As you can see here, they identified 176 patients, and 51 received placebo, while 50 were randomized to pioglitazone.

50
01:16:36,086 --> 01:16:44,782
They had this first primary outcome evaluating after 18 months when they repeated the liver biopsy.

51
01:16:44,782 --> 01:16:53,478
During that time, the patients who were in the placebo group, they were switched to pioglitazone, while the patients who were in pioglitazone continued for another 18 months to a total of 36 months.

52
01:16:53,478 --> 01:17:02,173
As you can see here, this was the primary outcome of the study, and they look at the NASH score after 18 months of pioglitazone.

53
01:17:02,173 --> 01:17:10,869
You can see on the right side of the graph, sorry, the left side of the graph, the primary outcome was a two-point reduction, two or more point reduction in NASH, and you can see it was 65% in the patients who received pioglitazone and 19% of the group was treated with placebo.

54
01:17:10,869 --> 01:17:19,565
Resolution of NASH was observed in 58% of the patients who were treated with pioglitazone.

55
01:17:19,565 --> 01:17:28,260
The liver biopsy histologic finds, you can see here, steatosis, inflammation, ballooning, similar to the prior study, also improved.

56
01:17:28,260 --> 01:17:36,956
You can see in fibros, most of the benefit was seen in the first 18 months, but they were sustained until the end of the study.

57
01:17:36,956 --> 01:17:45,652
In summary for those randomized controlled trials, pioglitazone targets insulin resistance, improves glucose metabolism, improves glycemia, improves liver, muscle, and adipose tissue insulin sensitivity, lowers liver enzymes, AST and ALT, reduces liver steatosis, both by imaging by histology, and also improves inflammation in ballooning.

58
01:17:45,652 --> 01:17:54,347
It seems to may reduce or at least prevent progression of fibros in patients with NASH.

59
01:17:54,347 --> 01:18:03,043
Going back to the history, unfortunately it wasn't that great after a few years after that.

60
01:18:03,043 --> 01:18:11,739
Rosiglitazone was found to increase risk of heart disease, and then it came out of the market in 2011, and this really affected the prescription of pioglitazone.

61
01:18:11,739 --> 01:18:20,434
But for the MASH we had some good news.

62
01:18:20,434 --> 01:18:29,130
Resmetron was approved last year, and the question now is pioglitazone still has a role for the treatment of MASH.

63
01:18:29,130 --> 01:18:37,826
So the main issue is the safety concern, so I want to go over a few of some of the adverse events that have been reported.

64
01:18:37,826 --> 01:18:46,521
So weight gain, peripheral edema, bone fractures, hypoglycemia, bladder cancer, heart failure.

65
01:18:46,521 --> 01:18:55,217
So all of those three, weight gain, it is seen in most of the studies, but it can be reversed, maybe combined with other drugs, or reinforcing a diet and lifestyle changes.

66
01:18:55,217 --> 01:19:03,913
Peripheral edema is seen in a very small number of patients, something that also patients can be informed, and maybe using lower dose you might be able to reverse that, or at least not see very severe.

67
01:19:03,913 --> 01:19:12,608
And bone fractures have been associated with treatment of pioglitazone, so you should measure bone density and avoid in patients who are at risk for osteoporosis.

68
01:19:12,608 --> 01:19:21,304
Hypoglycemia usually is not an adverse event from pioglitazone, but usually if you use in combination with the lower drugs, lowering sugars like insulin and sulfonylureas, so you need to be careful, but this is true for most of the drugs that we use in combination.

